WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.011
Welcome to Module 4 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.011 --> 00:00:05.675
set the credential rules.

3
00:00:05.675 --> 00:00:08.950
Module 3 decided where
each identity ends up.

4
00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:12.682
This module sets the rules for the
credentials they hold on the way:

5
00:00:12.682 --> 00:00:15.599
what the tenant will refuse,
and how a certificate is

6
00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:18.788
renewed and replaced without
anyone having to remember.

7
00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:23.349
It's Gate 3, Credential rules, with
Chapter 4 of the developer workbook,

8
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:26.950
on app management policies
and certificate rotation.

9
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:30.314
You'll compare a tenant's
policy with a reviewed file,

10
00:00:30.314 --> 00:00:35.087
plan a rotation from renewal to removal,
and approve each write by its hash.

11
00:00:35.087 --> 00:00:39.475
The code runs on your laptop, against
a fictional tenant and key vault.

12
00:00:39.475 --> 00:00:44.840
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

13
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:49.902
Why does this need code?

14
00:00:49.902 --> 00:00:52.240
The program's credential rules are short.

15
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.065
No long-lived secrets.

16
00:00:54.065 --> 00:00:58.965
Managed identities first, then federated
credentials, then certificates.

17
00:00:58.965 --> 00:01:02.702
And every certificate in a vault
and on a rotation calendar.

18
00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:05.277
Those rules are the
program's design guidance.

19
00:01:05.277 --> 00:01:09.642
The tenant can enforce part of that
itself, with an application management

20
00:01:09.642 --> 00:01:14.428
policy that blocks new secrets and caps
new certificates at a hundred eighty days.

21
00:01:14.428 --> 00:01:15.902
The rest needs code.

22
00:01:15.902 --> 00:01:18.971
Key Vault can renew a
certificate on a schedule,

23
00:01:18.971 --> 00:01:22.353
but Entra I D renews nothing
on the app registration.

24
00:01:22.353 --> 00:01:26.577
Somebody has to put the new certificate
on the app, and take the old one off.

25
00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:30.668
So in this module you compare the
policy with the tenant, and you plan

26
00:01:30.668 --> 00:01:35.330
a rotation from renewal to removal,
with each write approved by its hash.

27
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.959
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:41.959 --> 00:01:44.642
which takes about sixty-five
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:44.642 --> 00:01:48.850
You'll describe the tenant default
policy and an app management policy,

30
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.417
and how an exception
overrides the default.

31
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:54.261
You'll explain why the
policy plan never drops

32
00:01:54.261 --> 00:01:57.404
or loosens a restriction
the tenant already holds.

33
00:01:57.404 --> 00:01:59.390
You'll name the steps of a rotation,

34
00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.793
from the owner's approval to
removing the old certificate.

35
00:02:02.793 --> 00:02:06.086
And you'll run both plans on
the fixtures, dry-run them,

36
00:02:06.086 --> 00:02:09.380
and see the write path refuse
a hash nobody approved.

37
00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:15.152
Before you start, finish Module 1, because
both plans read its register, and Module

38
00:02:15.152 --> 00:02:20.493
3, because a secret isn't rotated: it's
replaced by the target Module 3 decides.

39
00:02:20.493 --> 00:02:25.529
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and a clone.

40
00:02:25.529 --> 00:02:26.830
No tenant.

41
00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.768
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 3, eight steps.

42
00:02:33.768 --> 00:02:34.867
Note three of them.

43
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:37.268
First, the policy's effective date.

44
00:02:37.268 --> 00:02:41.058
The restrictions bind every app
created after a date you choose,

45
00:02:41.058 --> 00:02:45.279
so new registrations follow the rule
while the old ones are fixed later.

46
00:02:45.279 --> 00:02:47.805
Second, the two rotation numbers.

47
00:02:47.805 --> 00:02:52.624
An existing workload moves onto a certificate
and is rotated at least yearly while

48
00:02:52.624 --> 00:02:55.137
it's being fixed, and
every new registration

49
00:02:55.137 --> 00:02:57.442
starts under the hundred eighty day cap.

50
00:02:57.442 --> 00:03:00.538
Third, closing the loop
between vault and directory:

51
00:03:00.538 --> 00:03:05.360
a renewed certificate in Key Vault doesn't
by itself change the credential on the

52
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.543
app registration, which is why Microsoft
Learn's procedure for rotating certificates

53
00:03:10.543 --> 00:03:15.005
on SQL Server enabled by Azure Arc
uploads the new one as a separate step.

54
00:03:15.005 --> 00:03:19.753
The other steps map the policy to three
policies in CISA's Scuba Gear baseline,

55
00:03:19.753 --> 00:03:23.187
which are should policies,
not required configurations;

56
00:03:23.187 --> 00:03:27.789
keep secrets by exception only; and
cover a group managed account's password,

57
00:03:27.789 --> 00:03:32.392
which Microsoft Learn's group managed
service account documentation says

58
00:03:32.392 --> 00:03:37.430
Windows changes every thirty days by
default, and new federated credentials.

59
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.742
Unit 2 reads the rules the code holds.

60
00:03:43.742 --> 00:03:47.310
Microsoft Learn's application
management policies

61
00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.229
A P I overview describes
two kinds of policy.

62
00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:54.700
The tenant default policy always exists,
is disabled by default, and holds

63
00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:59.830
separate restrictions for application
objects and for service principal objects.

64
00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.718
The program sets the same
restrictions on both.

65
00:04:02.718 --> 00:04:07.480
An app management policy is assigned
to one app, and an app takes only one.

66
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.767
It overrides the default for
the restrictions it defines,

67
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:13.918
and anything it doesn't define
falls back to the default.

68
00:04:13.918 --> 00:04:17.464
The program's design guidance
uses it for an exception:

69
00:04:17.464 --> 00:04:21.405
documented, time-limited, with
a record number and an end date.

70
00:04:21.405 --> 00:04:22.917
Two facts to remember.

71
00:04:22.917 --> 00:04:25.954
Neither kind blocks tokens
for an existing app;

72
00:04:25.954 --> 00:04:29.768
only the create or update that
breaks the policy is refused.

73
00:04:29.768 --> 00:04:35.293
And Microsoft Learn's Workload I D F A
Q lists app management policies in both

74
00:04:35.293 --> 00:04:40.930
the free edition and Workload I D
Premium, so this module needs no Premium.

75
00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.170
The policy check reads the default
policy, every app management policy,

76
00:04:49.170 --> 00:04:53.889
and each app registration, and compares
the default with the reviewed file,

77
00:04:53.889 --> 00:04:56.705
restriction by restriction,
on both objects.

78
00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:59.692
Add or Change means the
tenant lacks the program's

79
00:04:59.692 --> 00:05:03.117
restriction or holds a weaker
one, so the program's goes in.

80
00:05:03.117 --> 00:05:06.780
Keep Stricter means the tenant
already holds something stronger,

81
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.043
and Keep Existing means a
restriction the file doesn't name.

82
00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:11.981
Both stay as the tenant has them.

83
00:05:11.981 --> 00:05:16.967
The body is merged, so the PATCH never
drops a restriction and never loosens one.

84
00:05:16.967 --> 00:05:19.316
The findings, N P I P
O L oh one to oh six,

85
00:05:19.316 --> 00:05:24.742
flag a restriction the tenant doesn't hold
yet, a policy outside the reviewed file,

86
00:05:24.742 --> 00:05:27.334
an expired or unassignable exception,

87
00:05:27.334 --> 00:05:31.950
and an app registration without the
full app instance property lock,

88
00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:36.770
which stops anyone adding a
credential to its service principals.

89
00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:44.970
Rotation, in the program's design, is
raised automatically before expiry

90
00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.495
and approved by the owner, so the app
never depends on someone remembering.

91
00:05:49.495 --> 00:05:53.870
The code joins Key Vault
and Entra I D in five steps.

92
00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:57.058
A request is raised
sixty days before expiry.

93
00:05:57.058 --> 00:05:59.595
An owner who is an
enabled member approves.

94
00:05:59.595 --> 00:06:02.692
Key Vault makes the next
version from its own policy,

95
00:06:02.692 --> 00:06:07.445
as Microsoft Learn's Key Vault certificate
creation documentation describes.

96
00:06:07.445 --> 00:06:10.895
The new certificate is added
to the app beside the old one.

97
00:06:10.895 --> 00:06:14.527
And once the owner records that
the workload signs in with it,

98
00:06:14.527 --> 00:06:17.432
and seven days have passed,
the old one is removed.

99
00:06:17.432 --> 00:06:22.769
The findings, N P I R O T oh one to oh
seven, flag a rotation due with no approval,

100
00:06:22.769 --> 00:06:27.757
a certificate with no vault binding,
one expired with no replacement,

101
00:06:27.757 --> 00:06:32.219
a refused approval, a lifetime
over the cap, and a client secret,

102
00:06:32.219 --> 00:06:36.590
which isn't rotated at all:
it's reported for replacement.

103
00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.815
This is the kit's design.

104
00:06:41.815 --> 00:06:44.218
One script can write in this chapter,

105
00:06:44.218 --> 00:06:48.302
and it refuses to run unless the
plan's hash is the approved one,

106
00:06:48.302 --> 00:06:53.028
the plan is for this tenant, cloud and
task and no more than seven days old,

107
00:06:53.028 --> 00:06:55.190
and each write is a declared call.

108
00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:56.815
Then it checks every body.

109
00:06:56.815 --> 00:07:02.012
The default policy PATCH may set only the
enabled flag and the two restriction sets,

110
00:07:02.012 --> 00:07:06.003
and, checked again against the
policy read just before the write,

111
00:07:06.003 --> 00:07:08.790
it never loosens or
drops a live restriction.

112
00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.578
The lock body is exact.

113
00:07:10.578 --> 00:07:15.348
An attach keeps every certificate
the app has and adds exactly one;

114
00:07:15.348 --> 00:07:17.603
a removal drops only the old one.

115
00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:19.781
The Key Vault create body is empty,

116
00:07:19.781 --> 00:07:23.127
so the rotation never
changes a certificate policy.

117
00:07:23.127 --> 00:07:25.127
Delete and put are never sent.

118
00:07:25.127 --> 00:07:30.164
Before each write it re-reads the target,
and skips the write if anything changed.

119
00:07:30.164 --> 00:07:33.680
And a live run is a person
signed in, never an app.

120
00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:38.155
Try it.

121
00:07:38.155 --> 00:07:43.288
Step one makes Module 1's register, and
step two sends Microsoft's samples to

122
00:07:43.288 --> 00:07:48.255
the fakes: nineteen pass, and the thirty-two
not run are the code's own tests.

123
00:07:48.255 --> 00:07:51.455
Step three lists what each
of the four tasks needs.

124
00:07:51.455 --> 00:07:55.780
The two reading tasks hold only read
permissions and Key Vault Reader.

125
00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:59.150
The two write tasks add
Application dot ReadWrite dot All,

126
00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.942
the policy write permission, and
Key Vault Certificates Officer.

127
00:08:02.942 --> 00:08:06.367
Step four compares the
policy and builds its plan.

128
00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:08.518
The tenant's default is disabled.

129
00:08:08.518 --> 00:08:12.692
The diff is six adds, one
change and one keep existing.

130
00:08:12.692 --> 00:08:16.752
The plan has ten actions, and
eight write: patch the default,

131
00:08:16.752 --> 00:08:20.304
create and assign one
exception, and lock five apps.

132
00:08:20.304 --> 00:08:21.480
Nothing is sent.

133
00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:25.646
Step five reads the diff: three
restrictions added on both objects,

134
00:08:25.646 --> 00:08:28.374
for apps created after November fifteenth,

135
00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:33.190
and the tenant's own custom password
restriction kept, in the PATCH body.

136
00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:40.065
Step six runs the policy plan three times.

137
00:08:40.065 --> 00:08:43.900
First, with the plan's own hash and
no Apply switch, it's a dry run:

138
00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:48.503
the write path runs its prechecks and
reports eight writes it would send.

139
00:08:48.503 --> 00:08:53.123
One PATCH of the default policy, a
policy create and its assignment,

140
00:08:53.123 --> 00:08:55.391
and five lock PATCHes, one per app.

141
00:08:55.391 --> 00:08:58.965
Then the same command with Apply
sends all eight to the fake.

142
00:08:58.965 --> 00:09:02.878
In Lab 1, Apply changes
only the fake, in memory.

143
00:09:02.878 --> 00:09:05.766
Then you run the plan
with a hash of all zeros,

144
00:09:05.766 --> 00:09:10.178
which nobody approved, and it refuses
to run a plan nobody reviewed.

145
00:09:10.178 --> 00:09:15.303
Pasted line by line, as the lab means,
the shell carries on after that refusal.

146
00:09:15.303 --> 00:09:19.004
That's the pattern for
every write in this path:

147
00:09:19.004 --> 00:09:22.670
a plan, a reviewed hash,
a dry run, then Apply.

148
00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.820
Step seven builds the rotation plan.

149
00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:32.730
It reads seven app registrations
and writes seven steps:

150
00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:36.557
two findings, three information
items, and three writes.

151
00:09:36.557 --> 00:09:37.733
Nothing is sent.

152
00:09:37.733 --> 00:09:39.545
Step eight reads the plan.

153
00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:42.937
Vendor Portal's certificate
is due within sixty days

154
00:09:42.937 --> 00:09:46.020
and an owner approved, so
it's renewed in Key Vault.

155
00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.971
HR Connector's new
certificate was verified,

156
00:09:48.971 --> 00:09:52.958
and the seven-day overlap has
passed, so the old one is removed.

157
00:09:52.958 --> 00:09:55.819
Deploy Bot gets its new
certificate attached.

158
00:09:55.819 --> 00:10:01.908
Three apps still hold secrets: Legacy
Reports, Mail Automation and Payroll Export.

159
00:10:01.908 --> 00:10:04.096
Those are replaced, not rotated.

160
00:10:04.096 --> 00:10:05.849
Step nine dry-runs the plan,

161
00:10:05.849 --> 00:10:09.358
three writes it would send,
then applies it to the fake.

162
00:10:09.358 --> 00:10:12.710
For Vendor Portal, Key Vault
created the next version,

163
00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:15.682
and the next plan attaches
it, after a new review.

164
00:10:15.682 --> 00:10:20.190
Step ten runs every test:
five hundred thirteen pass.

165
00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.351
Lab 2 runs both plans in your own
test tenant, worldwide or G C C,

166
00:10:28.351 --> 00:10:33.644
with a test key vault that uses Azure
role-based access, never production,

167
00:10:33.644 --> 00:10:37.878
with fictional people, and with
Module 1's Lab 2 already run.

168
00:10:37.878 --> 00:10:42.192
Apply the policy from a test copy of
the reviewed file, then check that the

169
00:10:42.192 --> 00:10:46.578
tenant really refuses a new client
secret and a twelve-month certificate.

170
00:10:46.578 --> 00:10:48.441
Those refusals are evidence.

171
00:10:48.441 --> 00:10:52.562
Then carry one test app
through a whole rotation:

172
00:10:52.562 --> 00:10:56.890
raise it, approve it, renew,
attach, verify, and remove.

173
00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.255
Sign in as a person with Cloud
Application Administrator

174
00:11:00.255 --> 00:11:03.340
and Key Vault Certificates
Officer on the test vault.

175
00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.351
To back it out, step eight is a
person's PATCH of the default policy

176
00:11:07.351 --> 00:11:11.740
back to the copy saved before, because
this code never loosens a policy.

177
00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.390
Then delete the test
objects and the output.

178
00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:16.890
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

179
00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.027
The federal requirement.

180
00:11:22.027 --> 00:11:27.540
Gate 3 meets NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three I A five,

181
00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.592
enhancement two, S C twelve and S C
seventeen, and the policies M S dot

182
00:11:32.592 --> 00:11:37.828
A A D five point five to five point
seven in CISA's Scuba Gear baseline.

183
00:11:37.828 --> 00:11:41.803
Those three are should policies,
not required configurations.

184
00:11:41.803 --> 00:11:44.252
The module's table maps the evidence.

185
00:11:44.252 --> 00:11:47.683
For configuration settings
and the Scuba policies,

186
00:11:47.683 --> 00:11:51.990
the default policy as it was before,
the diff, and the reviewed file.

187
00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.227
For authenticator management,
the policy findings.

188
00:11:55.227 --> 00:11:58.446
For the certificates
themselves, the rotation plan,

189
00:11:58.446 --> 00:12:01.515
the Key Vault bindings
and the rotation findings.

190
00:12:01.515 --> 00:12:06.665
For change control, each plan's approved
hash and the dry-run and apply folders.

191
00:12:06.665 --> 00:12:09.865
And for least privilege,
each task's permission list.

192
00:12:09.865 --> 00:12:13.078
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty
X indicators for providers,

193
00:12:13.078 --> 00:12:16.810
and which file supports which
is the program's mapping.

194
00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.535
Check yourself with two questions.

195
00:12:22.535 --> 00:12:27.498
First: the tenant already holds a
restriction the reviewed file doesn't name.

196
00:12:27.498 --> 00:12:28.759
What does the plan do?

197
00:12:28.759 --> 00:12:29.972
The answer is c.

198
00:12:29.972 --> 00:12:32.761
It keeps it as the tenant
has it, Keep Existing.

199
00:12:32.761 --> 00:12:35.928
The PATCH never drops or
loosens a live restriction,

200
00:12:35.928 --> 00:12:40.497
and you saw that in step five, with the
tenant's custom password restriction.

201
00:12:40.497 --> 00:12:43.898
Second: Key Vault renewed
a certificate on its own.

202
00:12:43.898 --> 00:12:45.022
What's still missing?

203
00:12:45.022 --> 00:12:46.247
The answer is b.

204
00:12:46.247 --> 00:12:49.818
The new certificate has to be
added to the app registration,

205
00:12:49.818 --> 00:12:53.824
with the owner's approval, and the
old one removed after the overlap,

206
00:12:53.824 --> 00:12:57.248
because Entra I D renews
nothing on the app registration.

207
00:12:57.248 --> 00:12:59.674
The module page has three more questions,

208
00:12:59.674 --> 00:13:02.520
each linked to the
section that answers it.

209
00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.095
To sum up.

210
00:13:07.095 --> 00:13:11.446
You compared a fictional tenant's
default policy with the reviewed file,

211
00:13:11.446 --> 00:13:15.724
applied a plan that adds the program's
restrictions and locks five apps,

212
00:13:15.724 --> 00:13:20.583
and carried three certificates through
renewal, attach and removal, on the fakes.

213
00:13:20.583 --> 00:13:25.913
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate 3 from
this module is the default policy as it

214
00:13:25.913 --> 00:13:30.678
was before, the diff, both plans with
their approved hashes, the findings,

215
00:13:30.678 --> 00:13:34.958
and the dry-run and apply folders
with their manifests, all dated.

216
00:13:34.958 --> 00:13:37.520
From Lab 2, add the tenant's refusals and

